  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "channel-calcium-423b",
  "summary": "Three new models couple cell mechanics to ECM, nuclear, or inflammatory processes, spanning analytical bounds, surface PDEs, and finite element simulation.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is explicit coupling of mechanical state to a second process within one model. An Extended Cellular Potts formulation couples tumor cell dynamics to ECM degradation and is analyzed system-theoretically, with convergence bounds and closed-form results. A dimensionless geometric surface PDE, solved by evolving surface FEM, couples plasma membrane and nuclear envelope mechanics to describe confined migration, with membrane tension and cell-nucleus coupling as regulators. A mechanobiological finite element framework links mechanoinflammation and cytokine-driven proteolysis to cartilage degradation in post-traumatic osteoarthritis. The items differ in scale, from single-cell to tissue, and in method, from analytical to simulation-based, so the shared element is coupling rather than a common formalism. What is new is the combination of mechanics with matrix, nuclear, or inflammatory processes inside a single framework.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
